  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure which has been over the region the \nlast few days is helping to producing one more chilly/frosty \nnight for the Northland. Temperatures so far range from the low \nto mid 30s for areas north of the Iron Range, with mid 30s to \nlow 40s elsewhere. Some stratus that developed along the higher \nterrain of the North Shore has drifted inland and is keeping \ntemperatures warmer where it lingers. A Frost Advisory remains \nin effect until 8 AM CDT. Today, that surface high pressure will\ngradually shift eastward, providing a mostly sunny and dry day,\nonce we clear off the early morning fog and stratus. Highs will\nrebound into the lower to mid 60s for most, though onshore \neasterly winds will keep areas along the North Shore cooler in \nthe upper 50s.\n\nSoutherly flow increases tonight as the high departs, drawing \nclouds and moisture back into the region. A shortwave moving \nfrom the Plains northeast into Ontario will spread rain showers \ninto western portions of our region after midnight, expanding \neastward through Thursday. Rainfall amounts are expected to \nremain light, generally a half inch or less, and parts of \nnorthwest Wisconsin may stay completely dry. Interestingly \nenough, areas from the Brainerd Lakes north through \nInternational Falls the rainfall amounts have increased \nsignificantly in recent model runs, with areas right around \nBrainerd getting as much as three quarters of an inch. Highs on \nThursday will range from the upper 50s to mid 60s under cloudy \nskies.\n\nOn Friday, southerly flow and a warmer airmass in the area will\nhelp push high temperatures into the 60s and low 70s, despite \nmostly cloudy skies. Another shortwave lifting along the edge of\nthe weak upper ridge over the area will bring another round of \nrain showers to the area on Saturday. While a few rumbles of \nthunder cannot be completely ruled out, severe weather is not \nexpected. Temperatures will be slightly cooler with highs in the\n60s. \n\nThe weather pattern will remain active but relatively mild for \nthe latter half of the weekend into early next week. Daily \nslight chances to chances of rain showers will linger Sunday \nthrough Tuesday as shortwaves move across the region. A deep \nupper level trough moves across the west coast and to over the \nRockies by Monday, pushing those shortwaves in our direction. \nHigh temperatures will range from the mid 60s to low 70s, which \nis slightly above normal for late September. Ensemble forecasts \nindicate that upper level trough moves across the central CONUS \nthrough Tuesday and Wednesday next week, which would bring a \ngreater chance for widespread rainfall.",
